mer slave states this  legislation takes a look  at the work of those 1890 schools  and the discrimination had taken place  with black farmers i used to run the  south carolina commission  for farm workers and  i can tell you i'm well aware  of the inequities that existed in that  system  this legislation tackles that issue  like no legislation we have had  in my lifetime  and i think that alone  makes it worthwhile but it doesn't stop  there by any means  this legislation also tackles the issue  that we've had over the last several  years  with rural hospitals  this legislation goes straight to the  heart of that matter  and allows help rural hospitals  especially those  who are taxed with having to respond to  covert 19.  this lesson has somewhere around 7  billion to assist these hospitals  that's why if you look you will see  76 percent of the american people  support this legislation  and then that number 71 of independents  support this legislation 60  of republicans support this legislation  this is bipartisan legislation it may  not satisfy the protestants  on both sides of the capitol but it  satisfies  republicans 30 republican  mayors wrote in support of this  legislation  to me it is the best piece of  bipartisan legislation that i've seen  here in a long long time  and with that i'll hear you assistant  speaker  catherine clark thank you so much  i share the speakers excitement over  this  transformative bill and i am so grateful  and honored to be  up here with chairman yarmouth and  chairman neal  and grateful for your work and  leadership and that of your committee  and your incredible staff  and to the majority leader and mr whip  uh thank you for shepherding this bill  through  and to our speaker you always put the  children first  you center our work and our values  around children  and women and children have been hurt so  so deeply during this pandemic  it has been revealed through this  pandemic the house of cards  that is the foundation for wo